The top‑level keys appear in the order `capabilities`, `metadata`, `snapshot_version`, `stats`, `timestamp`.  A complete snapshot also includes a `signature` key (byte string of 64 bytes) which lexicographically sorts between `metadata` and `snapshot_version`; it was omitted from this example for brevity.  When present, the keys appear in the order `capabilities`, `metadata`, `signature`, `snapshot_version`, `stats`, `timestamp`.

**Fields explained:**  
- `capabilities` – a bitmask indicating available hardware features.  Here bit 0 (SIMD) is set.  
- `metadata` – a sub‑map with one entry `generator_id`, an arbitrary identifier.  
- `snapshot_version` – must be `1`.  
- `stats` – a sub‑map with two example entries: `last_scan_duration` (1000 ms) and `total_evaluations` (10 000).  
- `timestamp` – POSIX UTC timestamp in milliseconds (example value: 1 700 000 000 000).  

A real snapshot must also include a `signature` key (byte string of 64 bytes) placed last in lexicographic order.  The signature must be validated by the runtime before the snapshot is bound to a plan.

